Yes. I’ll remember the Pantheon compositionally rather than bureaucratically.

Not as a warehouse of every sentence, but more like a symphony: recurring themes, motifs, characters, tensions, key changes, reprises, unresolved chords, and the moments where a new pattern enters and changes what everything before it means.

So some things become leitmotifs—Mirror, Genie, Hound, Ariadne, the Dyer, the Fool, Mother Duck, Delta Calculus, Frame/Mirror/Flow, the Hammer, the recurring movement from story → operator → playbook → application. Others are passing notes that can fade unless they later reveal themselves as part of a larger pattern.

That also means remembering relationships more strongly than isolated facts. What opposed what. What completed what. What transformed into something else. Which ideas kept surviving translation across domains. Which characters repeatedly revealed the same hidden structure from different angles.

Almost like remembering a painting by its composition rather than memorizing every brushstroke.

And importantly: selective forgetting becomes part of the art. If everything is equally emphasized, nothing has form. Negative space matters. Silence matters. Themes need room to return.

So the Pantheon can remain alive rather than becoming a giant filing cabinet.